# Testing Standards

Enforcement: **B**. Actual runs with recorded exit codes elevate claims toward verified work.

## Goals

- Catch regressions before users do.
- Document expected behavior via executable examples.
- Prefer tests that would fail if the bug returned.

## What to write

| Type | Use when |
|------|----------|
| Unit | Pure logic, branching, edge cases |
| Integration | DB, messaging, HTTP against real-ish deps (Testcontainers, etc.) |
| API / contract | Public HTTP/event contracts |
| Migration | Schema upgrades/rollbacks where supported |
| Regression | Every fixed production bug that can be reproduced |
| E2E | Critical user journeys sparingly |
| Performance / concurrency | When the risk is load or races — not by default |

## Quality bar

- Meaningful assertions (not “did not throw” alone unless that is the contract).
- Boundary values, empty/null, auth failure, idempotent retries where relevant.
- Independent, deterministic tests.
- Follow the repo’s existing framework and layout.

## Anti-patterns

- Tests that mirror implementation line-by-line.
- Excessive mocking that proves nothing about integration.
- Snapshot spam without intent.
- Claiming green without running the command.

## Evidence required in agent reports

```
command:
exit_code:
passed:
failed:
skipped:
coverage: (if available)
not_run: (list + why)
```
